<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec_1_2">
    <title>The Graph of a Function</title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                After completing this section, you should be able to do the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Sketch or interpret the graph of a function given algebraically, numerically, or graphically, including in applied contexts.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Identify the interval(s) on which a function is increasing or decreasing from its graph.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the Vertical Line Test to determine whether a given curve is the graph of a function.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            One way to visualize a function is by graphing it.
            The graph of a function <m>y=f(x)</m> is the set of all ordered pairs or points <m>(x,f(x))</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane such that <m>x</m> is in the domain of the function <m>f</m> and <m>y=f(x)</m> is the output assigned to the input <m>x</m> by <m>f</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            This graph is often a curve in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
            Specifically, it is the set of all ordered pairs <m>(x,f(x))</m> for which the second coordinate <m>y</m> is the value of the function <m>f</m> at the first coordinate <m>x</m>; that is, the graph is the set of all points <m>(x,y)</m> that satisfy the equation <m>y=f(x)</m>:
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="jsx_1_2_function_graph">
            <caption/>
            <interactive platform="jsxgraph" source="jsxgraph/jsx_1_2_function_graph.js" width="60%">
                <slate xml:id="jsx_1_2_function_graph_fig" surface="jsxboard"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        Drag the point <m>x</m> to see how <m>(x,y)</m> changes.
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>
        <!-- <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_1">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="fig_1_2_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-1,xmax=3.5,
ymin=-1,ymax=3,
xtick={2.2},
xticklabels={$x$},
ytick={1.528},
yticklabels = {$f(x)$},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\)
]
\addplot[domain=-2:3] {x^3-3*x^2+2*x+1} node[left,pos=0.85] {$y=f(x)$};
\draw[dashed] (0,1.528) DASH-DASH (2.2,1.528) DASH_DASH (2.2,0);
\node[rpoint with label={0:{$(x,f(x))$}}] at (axis cs:2.2,1.528) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image> -->
        <p>
            It is not always reasonable or even possible to plot the ordered pair associated with each <m>x</m> in the domain of a given function <m>f(x)</m>.
            This is because functions can have many (sometimes even <em>infinitely</em> many) possible inputs.
            When drawing the graph of a function by hand, we customarily take relatively few points <m>x</m>, plot the corresponding ordered pairs <m>(x,f(x))</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, and then join neighboring ordered pairs with straight line segments.
            The result is an approximation of the graph of the function.
            Graphing calculators and other graphing tools do exactly this <mdash/> they just use a greater number of inputs <m>x</m> than we typically would when graphing by hand.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is useful to state a precise definition of the graph of a function as similar definitions appear in other contexts in mathematics.
        </p>

        <definition>
            <title>Graph of a Function</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    The graph of a function <m>y=f(x)</m> is the collection of all points <m>(x,y)</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane for which <m>y=f(x)</m>.
                    In other words, the graph is the collection of points <m>(x,f(x))</m> for all inputs <m>x</m> in the domain of <m>f</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="example-ch1-plot-points">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Sketch the graph of the function <m>y=f(x)</m> where <m>f(x)=0.2x^{2}</m> by hand.
                    Plot points on the graph for <m>x=0,\pm 1, \pm 2, \pm 3, \pm 4</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The below table gives each input <m>x</m>, the corresponding output <m>y</m> obtained by calculating <m>f(x)</m>, and then the associated point <m>(x,f(x))</m>.
                </p>

                <tabular left="minor">
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>y=f(x)</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(x,f(x))</m> pair</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{-4}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(-4)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{3.2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(-4,3.2)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{-3}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(-3)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{1.8}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(-3,1.8)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{-2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(-2)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{0.8}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(-2,0.8)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{-1}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(-1)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{0.2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(-1,0.2)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{0}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(0)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{0}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(0,0)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{1}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(1)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{0.2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(1,0.2)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(2)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{0.8}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(2,0.8)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{3}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(3)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{1.8}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(3,1.8)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>\textcolor{blue}{4}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>0.2\cdot(4)^{2}=\textcolor{red}{3.2}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(4,3.2)</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>

                <p>
                    Plotting these points on the <m>xy</m>-plane and joining neighboring points by straight line segments results in the following rough sketch of <m>f(x)=0.2x^{2}</m>:
                </p>

                <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_2">
                    <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="fig_1_2_2_fig">
   \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
    \begin{axis}[grid=both,
    xmin=-5,xmax=5,
    ymin=-1,ymax=4,
    xtick={-4,...,4},
    ytick={1,2,3,4},
    minor tick={},
    xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
    x post scale = 1.2
    ]
    \addplot+[sharp plot,mark=*,mark options={color=red}] plot coordinates {
    (-4, 3.2)
    (-3, 1.8)
    (-2, 0.8)
    (-1, 0.2)
    (0, 0)
    (1, 0.2)
    (2, 0.8)
    (3, 1.8)
    (4, 3.2)
    };
    \end{axis}
    \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>

                <p>
                    This is a decent approximation of the graph of <m>y=0.2x^{2}</m>.
                    A better graph of <m>y=0.2x^{2}</m> can be obtained from a graphing calculator, graphing software package, or other similar tool.
                </p>

                <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_3">
                    <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="fig_1_2_3_fig">
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                    \begin{axis}[grid=both,
                    xmin=-5,xmax=5,
                    ymin=-1,ymax=3.5,
                    xtick={-4,...,4},
                    ytick={1,2,3,4},
                    minor tick={},
                    xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
                    x post scale = 1.2
                    ]
                    \addplot[domain=-4:4] {0.2*x^2} node[left,pos=1] {$y=0.2x^2$};
                    \end{axis}
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            In <xref ref="example-ch1-plot-points"/>, the function <m>f(x)</m> was given <em>algebraically</em>.
            That is, we were provided the algebraic formula <m>f(x)=0.2x^{2}</m> for the function.
        </p>

        <p>
            Sometimes, a function is given <em>graphically</em>.
            That is, we are given only the graph of the function.
            The following examples illustrate that a great deal of information can be extracted from the graph of a function.
        </p>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The amount of nicotine in a person's bloodstream, <m>N=f(t)</m>, in milligrams, is a function of the time <m>t</m>, in hours, that has passed since the person smoked a single cigarette.
                    The graph of the function <m>f(t)</m> is given in <xref ref="fig-ch1-cigarette"/>.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ch1-cigarette">
                    <caption/>
                    <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_4">
                        <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image label="fig_1_2_4_fig">
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                        \begin{axis}[grid=both,
                        xmin=0,xmax=7.5,
                        ymin=0,ymax=2.5,
                        xtick={0,...,8},
                        ytick={0.5,1,1.5,2},
                        minor tick={},
                        xlabel=\(t\), ylabel=\(N\),
                        x post scale = 1
                        ]
                        \addplot[domain=0:7] {2*(0.5)^(x/2)};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>How much nicotine is absorbed from a single cigarette?</li>
                        <li>How much nicotine is left in the person's bloodstream <m>2</m> hours after smoking a single cigarette? What about after <m>4</m> hours?</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Note that <m>f(0)=2</m> (as the point <m>(0,2)</m> belongs to the graph of <m>f(t)</m> ). Thus, at <m>0</m> hours <mdash/> so immediately after a person finishes smoking a cigarette <mdash/> the amount of nicotine in their bloodstream is <m>2</m> milligrams. This is the amount absorbed from the cigarette.</li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                As time goes on, the amount of nicotine decreases.
                                We see from the grid on the <m>tN</m>-plane that <m>f(2)=1</m>; in other words, the point <m>(2,1)</m> lies on the graph.
                                This tells us that <m>2</m> hours after smoking a single cigarette there is <m>1</m> milligram of nicotine left in the bloodstream.
                            </p>

                            <p>
                                Similarly, we observe that <m>f(4)=0.5</m> and conclude that <m>4</m> hours after smoking a single cigarette there is only <m>0.5</m> milligram of nicotine left in the bloodstream.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-ch1-driving">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A woman is driving to visit with her family in a town <m>120</m> miles from her home.
                    Let <m>t</m> be the time, in hours, since she left her home.
                    Let <m>d</m> be the distance, in miles, to her destination.
                    Here, <m>d</m> is a function of <m>t</m>: <m>d=g(t)</m>.
                    The graph of the function <m>g(t)</m> is given in <xref ref="fig-ch1-driving"/>.
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ch1-driving">
                    <caption/>
                    <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_5">
                        <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image label="fig_1_2_5_fig">
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                        \begin{axis}[grid=both,
                        xmin=0,xmax=4.5,
                        ymin=0,ymax=6.5,
                        xtick={1,2,3,4},
                        ytick={1,2,3,4,5,6},
                        yticklabels={20,40,60,80,100,120},
                        minor tick={},
                        xlabel=\(t\), ylabel=\(d\),
                        x post scale = 1
                        ]
                        \addplot[domain=0:3] {6-2*x};
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>What is her distance from her destination <m>1</m> hour after she leaves home?</li>
                        <li>Estimate the time at which her distance from her destination is <m>60</m> miles.</li>
                        <li>When will she reach her destination?</li>
                        <li>How fast is she driving?</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>The point on the graph above <m>t=1</m> is <m>(1,80)</m>. In function notation, this can be written as <m>g(1)=80</m>. The practical meaning of this notation is that the distance to her destination after <m>1</m> hour of driving is <m>80</m> miles.</li>
                        <li>We look for the point on the graph for which the second coordinate is <m>d=60</m>. The <m>t</m>-coordinate of that point seems to be at <m>t=1.5</m>. We conclude that the point <m>(1.5,60)</m> lies on the graph, and that the associated function notation is <m>g(1.5)=60</m>. This means that after <m>1.5</m> hours, the woman is 60 miles from her destination.</li>
                        <li>The woman reaches her destination when <m>d=0</m>; that is, when <m>g(t)=0</m>. From the graph, it is clear that <m>g(3)=0</m>. Hence the woman arrives at her destination after <m>3</m> hours.</li>
                        <li>In the first hour of driving, her distance from her destination drops from <m>120</m> miles to <m>80</m> miles. After the next hour, it drops from <m>80</m> miles to <m>40</m> miles, and then from <m>40</m> miles to <m>0</m> miles during the hour after that. This pattern reveals that the woman is traveling at <m>40</m> miles per hour.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
        <title>Graphs of Increasing and Decreasing Functions</title>

        <p>
            In graphical terms:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>A function is increasing if its graph <alert>climbs</alert> as the independent variable increases; that is, as we move from left to right.</li>
                <li>A function is decreasing if its graph <alert>falls</alert> as the independent variable increases; that is, as we move from left to right.</li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            The function <m>N=f(t)</m> in <xref ref="example-ch1-plot-points"/> is decreasing <mdash/> its graph is falling as <m>t</m> increases.
            The amount of nicotine decreases as the amount of time after smoking a cigarette increases.
            In <xref ref="example-ch1-driving"/> the distance to the destination <m>d=g(t)</m> decreases as the time <m>t</m> spent driving increases.
            The function in <xref ref="example-ch1-plot-points"/> is neither increasing nor decreasing in its domain.
            At first, over the negative <m>x</m>-axis the graph is falling; the values <m>f(x)</m> decrease as <m>x</m> increases.
            Then, over the positive <m>x</m>-axis, the graph is climbing; the values <m>f(x)</m> increase as <m>x</m> increases.
            We can say that <m>f</m> is decreasing on the interval <m>x \lt 0</m> and increasing on the interval <m>x \gt 0</m>.
        </p>
        <aside>
            <p>
                We typically require that these intervals <m>I</m> be <em>open</em>: <m>(\cdot, \cdot)</m>.
                Why?
                You'll see in calculus.
            </p>
        </aside>

        <definition>
            <title>Functions Increasing or Decreasing on Intervals</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>A function <m>f(x)</m> is increasing on an interval <m>I</m> if the values <m>f(x)</m> increase as <m>x</m> increases along <m>I</m>.</li>
                        <li>A function <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing on an interval <m>I</m> if the values <m>f(x)</m> decrease as <m>x</m> increases along <m>I</m>.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Identify the interval(s) on which the function <m>f(x)</m> depicted below is increasing and the interval(s) on which it is decreasing.
                </p>

                <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_6">
                    <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="fig_1_2_6_fig">
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                    \begin{axis}[grid=both,
                    xmin=-4.5,xmax=4.5,
                    ymin=-5.5,ymax=5.5,
                    xtick={-4,...,4},
                    ytick={-5,...,5},
                    % yticklabels={20,40,60,80,100,120},
                    minor tick={},
                    xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
                    x post scale = 1.2,
                    y post scale = 1.2
                    ]
                    \addplot[domain=-4:4] {0.30303*x^3-3.71212*x};
                    \end{axis}
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The graph of <m>f(x)</m> is climbing on the interval <m>-4 \lt x \lt -2</m> and on the interval <m>2 \lt x \lt 4</m>.
                    Hence, the function is increasing on these intervals.
                    In interval notation, we would write that the function is increasing on <m>(-4,-2)\cup(2,4)</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The graph of <m>f(x)</m> is falling and thus <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing on the interval <m>-2 \lt x \lt 2</m>.
                    In interval notation, we would write that the function is decreasing on <m>(-2,4)</m>.
                </p>
                <aside>
                    <p>
                        Note that we do not use a bracket <m>[</m> or <m>]</m> around any of the endpoints <m>x=-4</m>, <m>x=2</m>, or <m>x=4</m> that appear in the interval notation here.
                        This is because a function is neither increasing nor decreasing at these endpoints <mdash/> it either <q>stops</q> at them, or <q>turns around.</q>
                    </p>
                </aside>
            </solution>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection>
        <title>Vertical Line Test</title>

        <p>
            We have established that the graph of a function <m>y=f(x)</m> is often a curve in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
            However, not every curve in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the graph of a function.
            The definition of a function requires that for each input <m>x</m> in the domain there is <alert>exactly one</alert> output <m>y</m>.
            That is, there cannot be two different outputs corresponding to the same input.
        </p>

        <p>
            The Vertical Line Test is a simple visual way of determining if a given curve is or is not the graph of a function.
        </p>

        <theorem>
            <title>Vertical Line Test</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let a curve in the <m>xy</m>-plane be given.
                    If there is a vertical line that intersects the curve more than once, then the curve does not represent a function.
                    If every vertical line intersects the curve at most once, then the curve represents the graph of a function.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
        <aside>
            <p>
                This test clearly requires having the <em>graph</em> of a curve.
                In the next section, when a table of values is given, we cannot use this test.
            </p>
        </aside>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the circle of radius <m>1</m> centered at the origin <m>(0,0)</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane the graph of a function?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Consider the vertical line corresponding to <m>x=0.5</m>; that is, the vertical line passing through the point <m>(0.5,0)</m> on the <m>x</m>-axis.
                </p>

                <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_7">
                    <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="fig_1_2_7_fig">
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                    \begin{axis}[grid=none,
                    xmin=-1.5,xmax=1.5,
                    ymin=-1.5,ymax=1.5,
                    xtick={-1,0.5,1},
                    ytick={-1,1},
                    xticklabel style = {xshift=0.5em},
                    yticklabel style = {yshift=0.5em},
                    minor tick={},
                    xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
                    x post scale = 1,
                    axis equal
                    ]
                    \draw[thick,blue] (0,0) circle (1);
                    \draw[thick,red] (0.5,1.5) -- (0.5,-1.5);
                    \node[rpoint with label={0:{}}] at (axis cs:0.5,0.866) {};
                    \node[rpoint with label={0:{}}] at (axis cs:0.5,-0.866) {};
                    \end{axis}
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
                <aside>
                    <p>
                        It takes only <em>one</em> vertical line intersecting the curve twice to conclude that the curve is not a function!
                    </p>
                </aside>

                <p>
                    This vertical line intersects the circle at two points with two different values of <m>y</m>, meaning there are two outputs corresponding to the input <m>x=0.5</m>.
                    This violates the definition of a function.
                    Hence, the circle is NOT the graph of a function.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            In utilizing the Vertical Line Test, it doesn't matter if the coordinates on the plane are labeled with <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, or other letters.
            The idea is the same.
        </p>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the graph of a straight line segment between the ordered pairs <m>(0,120)</m> and <m>(3,0)</m> in the <m>td</m>-plane the graph of a function?
                </p>

                <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_8">
                    <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="fig_1_2_8_fig">
   \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
    \begin{axis}[grid=both,
    xmin=0,xmax=3.5,
    ymin=0,ymax=6.5,
    xtick={1,2,3,4},
    ytick={1,2,3,4,5,6},
    yticklabels={20,40,60,80,100,120},
    minor tick={},
    xlabel=\(t\), ylabel=\(d\),
    x post scale = 1
    ]
    \addplot[domain=0:3] {6-2*x};
    \draw[thick,red] (0.5,6) -- (0.5,0);
    \draw[thick,red] (1.5,6) -- (1.5,0);
    \draw[thick,red] (2.7,6) -- (2.7,0);
    \end{axis}
    \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The line segment <em>is</em> the graph of a function whose domain is the interval <m>0\leq t\leq3</m>.
                    Using the Vertical Line Test, we can see that each individual vertical line that passes through a specific value of <m>t</m> with <m>0\leq t\leq 3</m> on the horizontal axis crosses the line segment at exactly one point.
                    Any vertical line that passes through a value of <m>t</m> outside the interval <m>0\leq t \leq 3</m> does not intersect the segment.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            Note that, in general, a function can have the same output for two different inputs.
            In <xref ref="example-ch1-plot-points"/>, the function <m>f(x)=0.2x^{2}</m> gives the same output <m>y=1.8</m> for both <m>x=3</m> and <m>x=-3</m>.
            Still, for each <m>x</m>, we have only one value of <m>y</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Having the same output for two different inputs means that some <alert>horizontal</alert> lines intersect the graph more than once which is perfectly fine for a function:
        </p>

        <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_2_9">
            <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image label="fig_1_2_9_fig">
   \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
    \begin{axis}[grid=both,
    xmin=-5,xmax=5,
    ymin=-1,ymax=4,
    xtick={-4,...,4},
    ytick={1,2,3,4},
    minor tick={},
    xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
    x post scale = 1.2
    ]
    \addplot[domain=-4:4] {0.2*x^2} node[left,pos=1] {\large $y=0.2x^2$};
    \addplot[domain=-4:4,red] {0.2*9};
    \end{axis}
    \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            We will revisit graphs of functions in Section 1.4.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <xi:include href="exercises/ex_1_2.ptx"/>
</section>